# Journaling repository

**Scope:** This rule applies to the **journaling** repo only. It is the canonical place for **architectural decisions** and **how to edit files** here. When adding features, update this file if behavior or layout changes.

## Layout

| Path | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `entries/YYYY-MM-DD.md` | One file per calendar day; hybrid capture (freeform + structured sections). |
| `reports/report-<audience>-<from>-to-<to>.md` | Period report (Markdown); see **Report audiences** below. |
| `reports/report-<audience>-<from>-to-<to>.slack.txt` | Same as **Slack mrkdwn** (`--format slack`) for posting. |
| `.cursor/skills/journal-capture-progress/` | Capture / append progress into daily entries. |
| `.cursor/skills/journal-generate-manager-report/` | Build manager report markdown from `entries/`. |
| `.cursor/skills/journal-post-slack/` | Post message to Slack (`chat.postMessage`). |
| `.cursor/agents/journaling-specialist.md` | Subagent: which skill to use and in what order. |
| `.cursor/skills/_shared/script_utils.py` | Token / `.env` helpers for stdlib scripts. |
| `.cursor/skills/_shared/journaling_repo.py` | Resolve repo root via `.cursor/rules/journaling-repo.mdc`. |
| `.cursor/skills/_shared/path_guard.py` | Keep file reads/writes inside this repo; size limits on reads. |
| `.cursor/rules/journaling-slack-formatting.mdc` | Slack mrkdwn rules for posted messages (not GitHub Markdown). |

## Entry schema (`entries/YYYY-MM-DD.md`)

- **Frontmatter** (YAML between `---` delimiters):
  - `date` (required): `YYYY-MM-DD`, must match the filename.
  - `tags` (optional): YAML list of strings, e.g. `[work, project-x]`.
  - `audience` (optional): e.g. `manager_update` when the capture targets manager reporting.

- **Body sections** (use these `##` headings so the report generator can parse them):
  - `## Freeform` — narrative; multiple captures the same day are appended as `### Capture <ISO8601>` under Freeform.
  - `## Wins` — bullet list (`- `).
  - `## Blockers` — bullet list.
  - `## Next Steps` — bullet list.
  - `## Metrics` — lines like `- name: value` or bullet key/value.

## Report schema (`reports/report-<audience>-<from>-to-<to>.*`)

- Generated by `journal-generate-manager-report` / `generate_manager_report.py`.
- **Captures** in `entries/` can stay **technical and detailed**; reports **reshape** the same fields (`Wins`, `Blockers`, `Next Steps`, `Freeform`, `Metrics`) per audience.
- **Markdown** (default): `.md` with `#` / `##` headings for local reading.
- **Slack** (`--format slack`): `.slack.txt` — [`.cursor/rules/journaling-slack-formatting.mdc`](journaling-slack-formatting.mdc).

### Report audiences (`--report-audience`)

| Audience | Intent | Shape |
|----------|--------|--------|
| `manager` | Leadership / exec-friendly | Short outcome paragraph from **Wins** + **Priorities ahead** (next steps); optional **Needs visibility** (blockers). Does **not** paste raw **Freeform** (keeps noise and jargon out). |
| `self` | Personal reflection | Full **Wins**, **Blockers**, **Next focus**, **Notes** (freeform from captures), metrics, sources. For Slack, optional `<@USER_ID>` via `JOURNALING_SLACK_USER_ID` or `--slack-user-id`. |
| `team` | Colleagues / shared context | **Highlights**, **Heads-up / dependencies**, **Coming up** — balanced for a channel. |
| `qa` | QA / validation | **Deliverables & changes**, **Risks / blockers**, **Suggested verification / follow-up**, optional metrics. |

Default: `--report-audience manager`.

## Date-range behavior

- **Inclusive** `--from` and `--to` (both ends included).
- Only days that have an existing `entries/YYYY-MM-DD.md` file appear in the report (missing days are skipped, not errors).

## Editing conventions

- **Do not** delete or overwrite a full-day entry without explicit user intent; prefer **append** via `append_journal_entry.py` or the capture skill workflow.
- Keep frontmatter valid YAML; preserve user wording in Freeform.
- **Secrets and install config:** store in repo-root `.env` (see `.env.example`). Never commit `.env`.
  - **Token:** `JOURNALING_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN` or any `SLACK_*` name (see `journal-post-slack` skill).
  - **Default channel:** `JOURNALING_SLACK_CHANNEL_ID` — used when `post_slack_message.py` is run without `--channel`.
  - **Optional user:** `JOURNALING_SLACK_USER_ID` — your member ID (`U…`); used with `post_slack_message.py --prepend-user-mention` and with `generate_manager_report.py --report-audience self --format slack` (prepend `<@…>`).
  - Before posting: invite the bot to the channel; app needs `chat:write` (see `.env.example`).

## Security and privacy (X-Team alignment)

Cross-check changes against **`standards/rules/hardcoded-secrets.md`**: no API keys, tokens, or passwords in source, tests, or committed config — only env vars / `.env` (gitignored). Placeholders in `.env.example` are fine.

Per **`standards/rules/sensitive-data-in-logs.md`**: scripts must not print request bodies, full Slack API responses, or token values. Status lines may name files or channel IDs only; avoid dumping user journal content to logs unless the user explicitly asked.

Per **`standards/rules/sensitive-data-in-errors.md`**: on failure, exit with a short generic message — do not echo stack traces, raw HTTP bodies, or token substrings to stderr.

**Path handling:** `--file` / `--freeform-file` / `--output` resolve **inside this repository** only (relative paths are anchored to the repo root). **SSRF:** Slack URL is fixed to `https://slack.com/api/chat.postMessage` (no user-controlled URLs).

**Slack posting:** `--channel` must be an ID (`C…` / `G…` / `D…`), not a `#name`. Message length is capped to Slack’s `chat.postMessage` text limit. Message **body** must follow [`.cursor/rules/journaling-slack-formatting.mdc`](journaling-slack-formatting.mdc) (mrkdwn, not GitHub Markdown).

## Slack message formatting

Canonical rules: [`.cursor/rules/journaling-slack-formatting.mdc`](journaling-slack-formatting.mdc). Use `generate_manager_report.py --format slack --report-audience <manager|self|team|qa>` when posting to Slack.

